glibc <= 2.22's configure hardcodes /bin/pwd in its "are we configuring in
the source directory?" check. The Nix build sandbox has no /bin/pwd (only
/bin/sh), so both command substitutions expand to the empty string, the two
"paths" compare equal, and configure aborts with the spurious

    configure: error: you must configure in a separate build directory

even though crosstool-NG always configures glibc in a separate build dir.
Upstream replaced /bin/pwd with `pwd -P` (shell builtin, POSIX) long before
2.23 -- which is exactly why the ubuntu16.04 sample (glibc 2.23) builds and
the centos7 (2.17) / ubuntu14.04 (2.19) samples do not. Backport the builtin.

Applies unchanged to the generated `configure` of glibc 2.17 and 2.19 (the
hunk context is identical; only the line offset differs, which patch(1)
handles). configure.ac is left alone: nothing in a ct-ng build regenerates
configure.

--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -3556,7 +3556,7 @@
 fi
 
 
-if test "`cd $srcdir; /bin/pwd`" = "`/bin/pwd`"; then
+if test "`cd $srcdir; pwd -P`" = "`pwd -P`"; then
   as_fn_error $? "you must configure in a separate build directory" "$LINENO" 5
 fi
 
